WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:02.580
So what is this history mode that it's yelling at us about?

00:02.580 --> 00:04.800
History mode is a way to basically define how you want

00:04.800 --> 00:06.600
your URLs to be displayed.

00:06.600 --> 00:08.560
Traditionally speaking, when you're looking at the view

00:08.560 --> 00:10.640
router docs, and we check it out here.

00:10.640 --> 00:12.840
So just so you know, traditionally, single-page

00:12.840 --> 00:15.200
applications, because they're just the index.html file

00:15.200 --> 00:17.480
when they're served to the web page, what's

00:17.480 --> 00:19.160
known as web hash history.

00:19.160 --> 00:21.960
So typically, what you would find is you would basically

00:21.960 --> 00:24.600
see the root page.

00:24.600 --> 00:27.640
And then it would say router.vjs.org.

00:27.720 --> 00:30.200
And then it'll do this hashtag symbol,

00:30.200 --> 00:33.240
and then it will basically do the routing from there.

00:33.240 --> 00:35.480
The problem with that is that it's not a true URL,

00:35.480 --> 00:37.440
and then there are SEO issues.

00:37.440 --> 00:39.680
And so what most of us are used to instead

00:39.680 --> 00:43.440
is what is termed HTML5 mode, or web history.

00:43.440 --> 00:46.360
And that is, in other words, how you would expect your URLs

00:46.360 --> 00:48.080
to normally look.

00:48.080 --> 00:51.320
And so basically, you need to define which one it is.

00:51.320 --> 00:54.180
So for us, we're going to go ahead and just use the web

00:54.180 --> 00:55.640
hash history right now, because there

00:55.640 --> 00:58.600
are some things you need to configure for the HTML5 mode

00:58.600 --> 01:00.840
that I'm not sure we'll have time to cover.

01:00.840 --> 01:05.680
So inside of our router, we have the history,

01:05.680 --> 01:10.840
and we would say the create web hash history function.

01:10.840 --> 01:15.360
And that can be imported from here.

01:15.360 --> 01:17.920
OK, now that we have this, this should start

01:17.920 --> 01:19.880
to feel a little bit better for the app.

01:19.880 --> 01:22.120
Oh, look, already something is showing up.

01:22.120 --> 01:25.840
Let me go back to app.view for our playground.

01:25.840 --> 01:27.880
And this time, let me go ahead and swap the color out,

01:27.880 --> 01:31.000
though, to white, so that it's actually easier to read.

01:31.000 --> 01:31.520
There we go.

01:31.520 --> 01:33.480
OK, now we're good.

01:33.480 --> 01:35.680
Now, you might notice, though, if we're looking here,

01:35.680 --> 01:38.400
that this is where the hash history is actually showing up.

01:38.400 --> 01:39.240
You see that?

01:39.240 --> 01:40.360
It's already starting to do that.

01:40.360 --> 01:43.400
But you might realize, wait, but didn't we

01:43.400 --> 01:44.240
define a home page?

01:44.240 --> 01:46.040
Like, why isn't that showing up?

01:46.040 --> 01:47.680
And the reason is because View Router

01:47.680 --> 01:49.160
provides us some new built-in components

01:49.160 --> 01:50.920
that's going to make all the difference when it comes to

01:50.960 --> 01:53.000
helping us manage the routing.

01:53.000 --> 01:57.240
So remember how in the SailorView, I was doing the,

01:57.240 --> 01:59.360
I was manually switching the view on stuff.

01:59.360 --> 02:02.800
So we go to app.view for SailorView.

02:02.800 --> 02:06.440
Inside of here, I had this component is that was dynamically swapping it out.

02:06.440 --> 02:11.800
Well, basically, View Router gives us a supercharged version of that,

02:11.800 --> 02:16.280
where we can actually just say, OK, inside of my app.view,

02:16.280 --> 02:22.600
I'm going to drop a thing called RouterView.

02:22.600 --> 02:26.440
And if you notice, my home page has now showed up.

02:26.440 --> 02:29.480
And if I switch over to Pokedex, OK, so we see some errors here

02:29.480 --> 02:30.760
regarding the Pokedex.

02:30.760 --> 02:33.320
And that is because it looks like it

02:33.320 --> 02:37.440
has to do with the fact that there is an asynchronous operation happening.

02:37.440 --> 02:39.600
So again, for the sake of demoing this real quick,

02:39.600 --> 02:42.920
because we want to focus on the routing part and not that,

02:42.920 --> 02:47.080
I'm just going to switch the H1 over to Pokedex real quick.

02:47.080 --> 02:48.360
And then we can see here, there we go.

02:48.360 --> 02:50.760
We have that here, and we go back home.

02:50.760 --> 02:53.320
That switches to the home page.

02:53.320 --> 02:57.040
And that, at its core, is how this all works, right,

02:57.040 --> 02:59.880
if we build it up from scratch.

02:59.880 --> 03:02.600
And so now with the RouterView, you might be thinking, well, OK,

03:02.600 --> 03:05.080
that's nice, but I don't have to memorize the links.

03:05.080 --> 03:06.640
That's not practical.

03:06.640 --> 03:09.120
I want actual links so I can switch it.

03:09.120 --> 03:11.560
And so you might think, OK, well, my natural instinct then

03:11.560 --> 03:14.360
is to create like a nav item, where I have an ahref,

03:14.360 --> 03:18.640
and I'm going to go to slash, Pokedex, and then Pokedex.

03:18.640 --> 03:23.960
And then I'll have one for the home, just like that.

03:23.960 --> 03:26.440
So that might be your first instinct.

03:26.440 --> 03:28.560
But if we do this and click on it,

03:28.560 --> 03:30.640
you'll notice that nothing's happening,

03:30.640 --> 03:35.400
because if we look up here, it's actually changing the URL to something

03:35.400 --> 03:36.880
that's not actually with the hash.

03:36.880 --> 03:39.640
You see this, what I mean, it's like blinking out.

03:39.640 --> 03:41.800
What you want it to do is actually be able to say,

03:41.800 --> 03:43.800
oh, it's actually on this side, because why?

03:43.800 --> 03:46.200
Because ViewRouter is the one responsible for managing that,

03:46.200 --> 03:48.720
not the browser.

03:48.720 --> 03:51.960
So don't worry, it's actually pretty easy to do.

03:51.960 --> 03:53.760
This is actually pretty easy to do.

03:53.760 --> 03:55.560
Rather than using the native anchor tag,

03:55.560 --> 03:57.040
what you end up getting instead is just

03:57.040 --> 04:01.160
like you have RouterView, you have a RouterLink component.

04:01.160 --> 04:04.000
And so that is ViewRouter's way of helping you to manage things

04:04.000 --> 04:06.800
like history, there's a lot of stuff you can do for you.

04:06.800 --> 04:08.880
But the main thing, though, that's

04:08.880 --> 04:12.600
different is that there is a two property rather than an href.

04:12.600 --> 04:15.840
Because again, it's not truly an anchor tag,

04:15.840 --> 04:18.080
it is doing some programmatic stuff underneath the hood

04:18.080 --> 04:20.720
to help you move to the desired pages.

04:20.720 --> 04:22.480
So I'll save that.

04:22.480 --> 04:25.000
And this time, when we swap over,

04:25.000 --> 04:27.600
you can see that it's actually doing it correctly.

04:31.040 --> 04:32.840
And so if we think about the RouterView,

04:32.840 --> 04:35.320
it really is just like the dynamic component.

04:35.320 --> 04:37.400
It's just swapping in and out components.

04:37.400 --> 04:40.640
And then we have that really nice user experience

04:40.640 --> 04:43.440
of having it already be basically automatically

04:43.440 --> 04:46.040
configured for us.

